WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:07.720
An electric fish senses the world with its own pulses.

2
00:00:08.740 --> 00:00:11.120
Why does every pulse not mask what it listens for?

3
00:00:12.083 --> 00:00:15.663
Each pulse also stimulates its passive electroreceptors.

4
00:00:16.423 --> 00:00:19.263
These only listen, for weak fields from outside sources.

5
00:00:20.333 --> 00:00:25.673
The command that fires the electric organ is also copied to the electrosensory lobe.

6
00:00:26.717 --> 00:00:29.717
Bell found that the effect of that copy is not fixed.

7
00:00:30.577 --> 00:00:32.817
It changed to match the input from the receptors.

8
00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:37.000
Now the key test. Paralyse the fish.

9
00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:41.900
The brain still issues the command, but the electric organ no longer fires.

10
00:00:43.033 --> 00:00:46.953
Pair each command with an artificial stimulus that mimics a pulse.

11
00:00:47.453 --> 00:00:49.153
Then switch the stimulus off.

12
00:00:50.117 --> 00:00:53.697
Predict. What does the command alone now do in the lobe?

13
00:00:59.750 --> 00:01:01.730
It evokes a mirror of the paired input.

14
00:01:02.590 --> 00:01:06.230
Where the stimulus excited the cells, the command alone inhibits them.

15
00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:09.500
This mirror is called a negative image.

16
00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:12.440
Added to the real input, it cancels it.

17
00:01:14.733 --> 00:01:18.253
It was learned, because it followed whatever stimulus had been paired.

18
00:01:21.283 --> 00:01:24.283
How does one command become a mirror with the right timing?

19
00:01:25.283 --> 00:01:28.143
Kennedy and colleagues recorded in awake, paralysed fish.

20
00:01:29.167 --> 00:01:32.427
Granule cells respond to the command at different delays.

21
00:01:33.447 --> 00:01:35.827
Unipolar brush cells add delayed responses.

22
00:01:36.950 --> 00:01:41.770
Together they form a temporal basis, a bank of signals each peaking at a different

23
00:01:41.770 --> 00:01:42.770
moment.

24
00:01:43.050 --> 00:01:44.590
Two rules set their strengths.

25
00:01:45.470 --> 00:01:49.710
An input followed shortly by a broad spike in the cell's dendrites is weakened.

26
00:01:50.833 --> 00:01:55.053
And every active input is strengthened a little, whether or not the cell fires.

27
00:01:56.167 --> 00:02:00.967
On average, each active synapse weakens or strengthens according to the broad

28
00:02:00.967 --> 00:02:02.507
teaching spikes that follow it.

29
00:02:03.227 --> 00:02:07.087
With enough differently timed inputs, learning shrinks the predicted response.

30
00:02:08.200 --> 00:02:10.520
So learning shrinks the response to the pulse.

31
00:02:11.320 --> 00:02:14.460
The command alone now dips where the pulse excited the cell.

32
00:02:17.433 --> 00:02:21.653
A model built on these recordings learned to cancel the predicted input.

33
00:02:22.593 --> 00:02:24.773
The limit: a paralysed fish, plus a model.

34
00:02:25.767 --> 00:02:27.107
Take this with you.

35
00:02:27.727 --> 00:02:32.167
Using a copy of the command, the circuit learns a timed negative image of the

36
00:02:32.167 --> 00:02:33.067
expected input.
